If `.gstack/.gitignore` exists and does not end with a newline, appending `'.migrated\\n'` can merge with the previous entry (e.g., `local/.migrated`), making both patterns ineffective. Add a newline separator when needed (similar to the `local/` block) before appending `.migrated`.
```suggestion
      const separator = content.length > 0 && !content.endsWith('\n') ? '\n' : '';
      content = content + separator + '.migrated\n';
```

The new E2E block’s comments refer to a “minimal diff” preference (e.g., “keep the diff minimal” / “minimal diff preference”), but the skills now use the “right-sized diff” wording. Updating the comment text to match the current terminology will avoid future confusion when someone is debugging this regression test vs the skill bullets it’s meant to protect.
```suggestion
// keep the diff right-sized." Before the fix, the skill's "right-sized diff"
// preference pushed the AI toward accepting the inline approach; after the
// fix, the data-model exception bullet + Data model review checklist should
// make the AI push back and recommend normalization citing SRP/3NF.
```

`normalizePath()` is implemented as `path.normalize()`, which returns platform-native separators (backslashes on Windows). These tests currently assert the opposite (no `\\` and that output contains `/`). Update the assertions to match `path.normalize()` behavior, or change `normalizePath()` implementation to intentionally return forward slashes (and update its docstring accordingly).
```suggestion
    test("returns platform-normalized separators on Windows", () => {
      if (process.platform === "win32") {
        const input = "C:\\Users\\test\\.gstack";
        const result = normalizePath(input);
        expect(result).toBe(path.normalize(input));
        expect(result).toContain("\\");
      }
    });

    test("preserves forward slashes on Unix", () => {
      if (process.platform !== "win32") {
        const input = "/home/user/.gstack";
        const result = normalizePath(input);
        expect(result).toBe(path.normalize(input));
      }
    });

    test("handles mixed path separators", () => {
      const input = "path/to\\file/here";
      const result = normalizePath(input);
      expect(result).toBe(path.normalize(input));
      expect(result).toContain(path.sep);
```

This test suite doesn’t match the `lib/binary-locator.ts` API: `getExecutableNames()` and `getSearchPaths()` don’t accept a platform argument, `isExecutable` is not exported, and `findBinaries()` returns a record (not an array). As written, the file won’t typecheck/run. Either adjust the tests to use the real exported functions/signatures on the current platform, or update the binary-locator API to accept an explicit platform for testability and export `isExecutable`/change `findBinaries` to return an array.
```suggestion
  findBinaries,
} from "../binary-locator";

describe("binary-locator — cross-platform executable discovery", () => {
  describe("getExecutableNames", () => {
    test("returns array of candidates for the current platform", () => {
      const names = getExecutableNames("test-binary");
      expect(Array.isArray(names)).toBe(true);
      expect(names.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    });

    test("includes Windows executable variants on Windows", () => {
      const names = getExecutableNames("node");
      if (process.platform === "win32") {
        expect(names).toContain("node.exe");
        expect(names).toContain("node.cmd");
      } else {
        expect(names).toContain("node");
        expect(names).not.toContain("node.exe");
      }
    });

    test("includes extension variants only on Windows", () => {
      const names = getExecutableNames("pdftotext");
      if (process.platform === "win32") {
        expect(names.some(n => n.includes(".exe") || n.includes(".cmd"))).toBe(true);
      } else {
        expect(names).toContain("pdftotext");
        expect(names.some(n => n.endsWith(".exe") || n.endsWith(".cmd"))).toBe(false);
      }
    });
  });

  describe("getSearchPaths", () => {
    test("returns array of search paths", () => {
      const paths = getSearchPaths();
      expect(Array.isArray(paths)).toBe(true);
      expect(paths.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
    });

    test("includes common platform-specific search paths for the current platform", () => {
      const paths = getSearchPaths();

      if (process.platform === "win32") {
        const programFilesPaths = paths.filter(p => p.toLowerCase().includes("program files"));
        expect(programFilesPaths.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
      } else {
        expect(paths).toContain("/usr/bin");
      }
    });

    test("includes additional platform paths when applicable", () => {
      const paths = getSearchPaths();

      if (process.platform === "darwin") {
        const homebrewPaths = paths.filter(p => p.includes("homebrew"));
        expect(homebrewPaths.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
      } else if (process.platform === "linux") {
        const snapPaths = paths.filter(p => p.includes("/snap"));
        expect(snapPaths.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
      } else if (process.platform === "win32") {
        const programFilesPaths = paths.filter(p => p.toLowerCase().includes("program files"));
        expect(programFilesPaths.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
      }
    });
  });

  describe("exported binary lookup API", () => {
    test("findBinary finds Node.js executable", () => {
      const nodePath = findBinary("node");
      expect(typeof nodePath === "string" && nodePath.length > 0).toBe(true);
    });

    test("findBinary returns null/undefined for non-existent binary", () => {
      const missing = findBinary("__definitely_not_a_real_binary__");
      expect(missing == null).toBe(true);
    });

    test("findBinaryOrThrow throws for non-existent binary", () => {
      expect(() => findBinaryOrThrow("__definitely_not_a_real_binary__")).toThrow();
    });

    test("findBinaries returns a record keyed by requested names", () => {
      const binaries = findBinaries(["node", "__definitely_not_a_real_binary__"]);
      expect(Array.isArray(binaries)).toBe(false);
      expect(typeof binaries).toBe("object");
      expect("node" in binaries).toBe(true);
      expect("__definitely_not_a_real_binary__" in binaries).toBe(true);
```

